WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:00.963
- [Sal] Is there a lag?

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:07.893
- [Producer] Okay, standby, here we go.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.850
- Hello, I think we are up now.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:23.950
So thanks for joining
our morning livestream

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.170
here at Khan Academy,
we're calling it something

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.750
of a homeroom, a national homeroom,

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.190
or an international homeroom I guess.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:34.670
And every day we are trying
different types of technology,

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:36.420
so we're trying a new technology today,

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.450
hopefully it works out, but
definitely bear with us.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:40.490
I think we've definitely
improved from earlier this week

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:43.780
when I was operating from
my not-so-great wifi.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:45.642
But things have improved.

00:01:45.642 --> 00:01:47.670
So thanks for joining, for those of y'all

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:49.360
that this is the first day you're joining

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:53.870
this homeroom, it's really a
way for all of us to connect.

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:56.890
As I'm sure many of you
know, and are experiencing,

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:59.690
we now have over 40
million students in America

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:02.759
who are now not in physical school,

00:02:02.759 --> 00:02:04.930
they are trying to learn from home.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:07.450
And we have approaching a
billion kids around the world

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:08.890
who are in that situation.

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:11.850
And over the last few, well
really over the last decade,

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:14.179
Khan Academy has
inadvertently building things

00:02:14.179 --> 00:02:17.580
that can hopefully help folks navigate

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:19.280
this school closure situation.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.201
This is a very suboptimal situation,

00:02:21.201 --> 00:02:24.910
but we have over the years created things

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:27.060
like Khan Academy Kids for early learners,

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.850
that's for ages three through six.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:30.610
Covers the head start
standards, all the way

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.140
through first grade, and
reading, writing, math,

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:34.420
social, emotional learning.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:37.340
And I guess you could say
the main Khan Academy,

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:40.700
whether you're on the
website, or you're on our app,

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:44.350
it goes for sure all the way
in math through elementary,

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:45.660
middle, and high school, and even some

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:46.920
of the core of college.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:48.910
A lot of people associate us with videos,

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:50.170
it's not just videos.

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:52.980
In fact, most of what we put resources to

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:55.410
are interactive exercises
that have solutions

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:56.250
to every problem.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.200
Students should never be able to run out

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.340
of practice, they get feedback,
there's game mechanics

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:03.430
to make sure they really
mastered the concept,

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:06.788
there's data and reporting
for teachers or parents

00:03:06.788 --> 00:03:10.500
if they wanna help the students progress.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.580
We've recently launched a Beta,

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:15.060
which is kind of a first
draft version of our

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.220
English and language arts.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:18.690
It's not quite as rich as
what we have on the math side,

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:21.780
but the exercise items,
I think, are quite good.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:24.360
That goes from second
through eighth grade.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:26.727
And then as you get into high school,

00:03:26.727 --> 00:03:28.470
not only do we have the math, but we also

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:31.720
have the sciences, biology,
chemistry, physics,

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:34.010
economics, computer science.

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:35.370
For those of you in middle school,

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.340
I actually highly recommend
high school biology,

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:39.510
I think you have the math
background to handle it,

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:40.950
and it's very relevant to the world

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:42.070
that we're in today.

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:44.340
And we also have SAT practice in reading,

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:45.815
writing, and math, and that's
an official partnership

00:03:45.815 --> 00:03:48.860
with the college board.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:51.420
And we have all of those resources,

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:54.520
and we realized last week
that we've got to be able

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:55.810
to put these things together in a way

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:57.960
that it feels more structured,

00:03:57.960 --> 00:03:59.460
in a world where so many students

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:00.790
are going to be working from home.

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:03.670
And so that's why over the past weekend

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:06.060
we launched the schedules on Khan Academy.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.550
There could be links,
depending on what social media

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:09.870
channel you're looking
at, you could also just

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:10.703
do a web search.

00:04:10.703 --> 00:04:12.410
If you just go to KhanAcademy.org,

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:14.500
we have banners that
lead you to the schedules

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:16.740
and other resources that we're having.

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:18.840
But that helps structure student's days.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:22.270
For young students,
middle school students,

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:25.470
high school students,
elementary school students,

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:27.430
and we've been getting a lot
of good feedback from that,

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:28.947
and we're constantly
tweaking it so it becomes

00:04:28.947 --> 00:04:30.080
better and better.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:31.330
So please give us feedback on that.

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:32.780
But the purpose of this homeroom,

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:36.077
that was a long parenthetical,
the purpose of this

00:04:36.077 --> 00:04:40.150
homeroom is to, as folks work
at their own time and pace,

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:43.590
on those types of schedules,
to feel a connectedness.

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:46.480
When you're in a real
school, there's a homeroom,

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:49.240
and there's announcements,
and we get to see each other.

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:52.000
We get to kind of prepare
our minds for the day.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.620
And so this is really for everybody,

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.580
it's for students, it's for parents,

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:58.312
it's for teachers, just as
a way to start their day.

00:04:58.312 --> 00:05:01.547
And in this time of social distancing,

00:05:01.547 --> 00:05:04.150
hopefully feel a little bit connected.

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:07.160
A couple of announcements,
as many of y'all know,

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:09.620
I've been mentioning this
in the last few livestreams,

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:12.260
Khan Academy is a
not-for-profit, with a mission

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:13.560
of a free world class education

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:14.570
for anyone, everywhere.

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:16.910
Not-for-Profit means no
one owns Khan Academy,

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:17.930
it's a public charity.

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:19.660
I don't own Khan Academy, you own as much

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:21.810
of Khan Academy as I do.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:25.160
And we are dependent on
philanthropic donations.

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:30.160
And we are proud, and we wanna step up

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:32.640
in every way possible to serve everyone

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:35.220
during this situation,
but what happens with

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:37.553
a not-for-profit like ours is our servers,

00:05:38.436 --> 00:05:41.331
our usage has more than
doubled as of the latest data

00:05:41.331 --> 00:05:43.490
that I saw from yesterday.

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:47.153
And that increases our
costs, and our funds

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:52.410
come from philanthropic donations
from folks like yourself.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:55.391
So we definitely need help.

00:05:55.391 --> 00:06:00.000
And so, let's see, I just,
looks like the screen just

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:00.900
clicked out on me.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:01.940
Oh, it's back.

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:03.515
All right, sorry.

00:06:03.515 --> 00:06:08.230
So we need help, but I
do wanna thank several,

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:09.680
there's many of y'all who've been donating

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:10.900
at all different levels.

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:12.550
I definitely wanna thank some corporations

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:15.250
who have stepped up in record time

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:16.130
over the weekend.

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:17.714
Bank of America was the first to step up

00:06:17.714 --> 00:06:20.150
when they found out our situation,

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:22.717
and how much the resources
we have to put out there

00:06:22.717 --> 00:06:25.470
and what we hope to do to get everyone

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:26.960
through this school closure situation.

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:28.130
And they encouraged us to think big,

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:29.811
so Bank of America gave
a very generous donation

00:06:29.811 --> 00:06:31.220
over the weekend.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:32.771
You can read up about that announcement,

00:06:32.771 --> 00:06:35.235
and just this morning,
I really wanna thank

00:06:35.235 --> 00:06:39.330
AT&amp;T and Google.org for
following close behind

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:43.010
Bank of America and also
stepping up with significant

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:46.090
support so that we can
handle all the extra

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:49.520
programs we wanna do, the server costs

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:53.960
that have roughly doubled
now, and we were frankly

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:55.680
already running at a deficit before this.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:58.750
'Cause you can imagine serving up content,

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:00.350
we have a team of over 200 folks,

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:03.910
and serving up content to
tens and what is likely

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:05.500
hundreds of millions of folks

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:08.450
now does take a lot of resources.

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:10.610
So thank you for everyone there.

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:14.200
So with that said, with
my asking for support

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:16.760
from the world, and we need more,

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:18.480
I'll turn to your questions.

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:22.203
So the first question,
and this is one we get

00:07:22.203 --> 00:07:24.540
pretty consistently is, as a parent,

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:26.730
how should I figure out what my child

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:27.860
should be learning?

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:29.930
So those schedules that we put out,

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:32.580
they have, and you should
definitely feel free

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:35.220
to modify those schedules
so they work for you,

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:38.800
and depending on what
works for your child.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:41.850
But in each of those areas, for say math,

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:44.130
we give some grade levels
that might be appropriate

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:45.970
for students of a certain age,

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:49.744
and I would say if your
student has historically

00:07:49.744 --> 00:07:53.330
felt confident in math and
has done quite well in math,

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:55.310
you can just start them
right at grade level.

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:57.300
They can take something
called the course challenge

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:58.970
that's right from the course page,

00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:00.180
and on that course challenge,

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:02.320
it'll take about 30 to 40 minutes,

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:04.040
they'll have to answer
about 30, 40 questions,

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:07.650
and then it will tell them
what they got right or wrong,

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:08.900
and then the things that they got wrong,

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:12.229
I would recommend going to
those units in the course.

00:08:12.229 --> 00:08:14.760
And on those units, if the student feels

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:16.450
like they know a good bit of that unit,

00:08:16.450 --> 00:08:19.140
they can go to the unit
test, and take that,

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:20.840
and then that'll give them
credit for what they know,

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:22.910
then they can focus on the
skills that they don't know.

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:26.020
And then the game mechanics
for the entire site

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:28.570
has unit test, course
challenges, other things,

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:31.530
mastery challenges, so
that it encourages kids

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:33.050
to show that they have mastery,

00:08:33.050 --> 00:08:34.720
that they can fill in all
those gaps in their knowledge,

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:38.654
that they can do the
skills in a mixed topic

00:08:38.654 --> 00:08:40.180
type of environment.

00:08:40.180 --> 00:08:41.540
So that's one method.

00:08:41.540 --> 00:08:43.410
If your child, or if you're a student,

00:08:43.410 --> 00:08:46.270
and maybe you've felt a
little bit weak in math,

00:08:46.270 --> 00:08:48.210
one thing that we've seen, Tim Vandenberg,

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:50.890
this teacher that I've been really, really

00:08:50.890 --> 00:08:52.910
impressed by over, I've heard about him

00:08:52.910 --> 00:08:55.000
a couple weeks ago, he actually starts

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:56.810
all of his sixth graders at kindergarten,

00:08:56.810 --> 00:08:59.050
and that's not to talk down to 'em,

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:01.500
it's actually 'cause he has
very high expectations for them.

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:03.310
What happens there is, the students,

00:09:03.310 --> 00:09:05.350
by starting at kindergarten, they're able

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:08.900
to build some momentum,
obviously a lot of sixth graders,

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:10.470
most sixth graders will get
through the kindergarten

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:12.440
content, they can do it
through the course challenges

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:15.956
in literally 30 to 40 minutes,
then they go to first grade.

00:09:15.956 --> 00:09:18.240
You can do all of early
learning on Khan Academy,

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:20.400
you can do arithmetic, and he
also makes all of his students

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:22.020
do the third grade, because he sees that

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:23.750
that has a lot of
foundational things in it,

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:27.310
like your basic arithmetic fluency,

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:29.680
and your basic multiplication
fluency, things like that.

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:31.390
And then he has them simultaneously

00:09:31.390 --> 00:09:32.260
working at grade level.

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:36.380
So I think this situation, might be,

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:38.540
especially if you have a
child, or you are a student

00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:40.480
who's historically struggled in math,

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:42.060
I recommend spending
more time so you can work

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:44.450
at grade level, and make
sure that you don't have

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:45.870
any Swiss cheese gaps going forward.

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:47.070
And then the same thing is true in English

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:48.160
and language arts.

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:49.760
You should always be working at a level

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:51.740
that you're getting a few questions wrong,

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:54.110
it's a little bit difficult,
but you can definitely

00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:56.800
engage on it, would be my best advice.

00:09:56.800 --> 00:09:58.294
And I think over the
next few days and weeks,

00:09:58.294 --> 00:10:01.880
because Khan Academy has
all of the grade levels,

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:03.787
it's a good time to try to triangulate,

00:10:03.787 --> 00:10:05.683
where is that learning edge?

00:10:05.683 --> 00:10:07.920
And you should be able to
get to it in about a week,

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:09.330
and then learn from there, and really

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:11.630
have strong foundations.

00:10:11.630 --> 00:10:15.777
So let's see, Kylie Kameoka says, "Hi Sal,

00:10:15.777 --> 00:10:18.097
"as a tutor, how do you figure out where

00:10:18.097 --> 00:10:19.820
"the students' gaps are?"

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:21.422
So a very similar question.

00:10:21.422 --> 00:10:26.300
If you really wanna go for gap finding,

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:29.240
you should start students
at the very basic material,

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:31.840
that's why we created
mechanisms on Khan Academy

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:33.350
to accelerate through it.

00:10:33.350 --> 00:10:36.330
But that's really the only
way to go skill by skill

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:38.160
and ensure that students have no gaps.

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:39.950
And what we see over and over again

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:44.091
is even students who are A
students in middle school,

00:10:44.091 --> 00:10:46.250
they still have a few
gaps, or they're still not

00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:48.630
completely fluent at certain
things from arithmetic,

00:10:48.630 --> 00:10:49.690
or the things that came before.

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:51.630
We see many high school
students who are not

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:53.480
fluent at middle school skills,

00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:55.380
or at elementary school skills,

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:56.400
and so it's really valuable.

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:58.830
It might seem like you're
taking a step back,

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:00.985
but it's kind of a go slow to go fast.

00:11:00.985 --> 00:11:05.000
If you take that week to
start from the beginning

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:06.363
and build up all your skills,

00:11:06.363 --> 00:11:08.270
then when you get to
the middle school math,

00:11:08.270 --> 00:11:11.650
or the algebra, you'll find
that you're in the right place.

00:11:11.650 --> 00:11:13.580
If you're looking at other
topics on Khan Academy,

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:17.740
like biology, or physics,
I'd advocate the same thing.

00:11:17.740 --> 00:11:19.181
Economics, take the course challenge,

00:11:19.181 --> 00:11:21.880
understand what you
know and you don't know,

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:24.020
and then go back and fill in those gaps

00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:26.370
in the appropriate units.

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:29.797
So Underground School says,
"Other than career preparation,

00:11:29.797 --> 00:11:33.047
"what is the role of learning in life?"

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:37.327
So you know, Underground School,

00:11:37.327 --> 00:11:41.490
it's an interesting name,
it's a deep question

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:44.746
of what is the purpose of life period?

00:11:44.746 --> 00:11:47.610
And you know, I think one of the things

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:50.720
that makes us as human
beings fairly unique,

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:52.370
we know what some of them are,

00:11:52.370 --> 00:11:56.890
we have opposable thumbs,
although all of our primate

00:11:56.890 --> 00:11:59.407
and ape cousins have opposable thumbs.

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:05.940
We have large brains, and
we walk on two legs upright,

00:12:08.090 --> 00:12:10.200
and we have language,
which is maybe a byproduct

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:11.420
of our large brains.

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:12.720
And maybe all those things come together,

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:15.570
the opposable thumbs, the
brain, and the language,

00:12:15.570 --> 00:12:17.900
and I think that learning
is one of the most

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:20.420
fundamental human things
that makes us feel human.

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:23.590
And so I've gotten letters over the years

00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:26.460
from Khan Academy, from people
who are past their career,

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:28.620
or well into their
career, and they realized

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:31.144
later in life how much joy there is

00:12:31.144 --> 00:12:32.810
just for the sake of learning,

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:34.470
just trying to understand this mystery

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:35.979
that we find ourselves in called life,

00:12:35.979 --> 00:12:38.070
understanding the
universe a little better.

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:41.950
And what I've said in
multiple livestreams is,

00:12:41.950 --> 00:12:43.860
some students have asked,
"How do I get motivated?"

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:45.620
And I remind them that this content

00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:46.930
that you can learn on Khan Academy,

00:12:46.930 --> 00:12:48.230
that you can learn in your textbooks,

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:51.920
a lot of what you see in a lesson or unit

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:54.230
is someone's life work,
or it's probably many

00:12:54.230 --> 00:12:56.000
of hundreds of people's life work.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:58.160
And they would've done anything to see

00:12:58.160 --> 00:12:59.670
that knowledge that you now see.

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:00.990
How does photosynthesis work?

00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:03.190
What does mitochondria do?

00:13:03.190 --> 00:13:07.690
Calculus, people like
Newton and Leibniz spent

00:13:07.690 --> 00:13:10.930
decades trying to derive these things,

00:13:10.930 --> 00:13:14.210
and you can now understand it
in a more distilled format.

00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:16.051
I think that's incredibly exciting.

00:13:16.051 --> 00:13:20.390
And it just changes your
perspective on everything,

00:13:20.390 --> 00:13:22.289
and it also just makes
you a better citizen.

00:13:22.289 --> 00:13:25.190
In a world where we want democracy,

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:26.960
we want people to be able to participate,

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:29.040
there's an obligation
of the person themselves

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:31.220
to be as informed as
possible, to be able to look

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:33.310
at the data that exists in the world,

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:35.150
to be able to think
critically about the world,

00:13:35.150 --> 00:13:38.400
and that's what education
is gonna provide.

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:40.400
You'll also be more fun at dinner parties,

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:42.780
better dinner party conversation.

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:45.177
All right, Kirsten Martin says,

00:13:45.177 --> 00:13:46.837
"How does a teacher use Khan Academy

00:13:46.837 --> 00:13:48.957
"to teach a class at multiple levels?

00:13:48.957 --> 00:13:52.020
"What should the teacher
component focus on?"

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:57.020
So Kirsten, if we weren't in
the school closure situation,

00:13:57.430 --> 00:13:58.890
the teachers that I've
seen use Khan Academy

00:13:58.890 --> 00:14:00.760
to great effect, and I've visited hundreds

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:02.720
of classrooms over the
years, and Tim Vandenberg's

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:05.190
the most recent that I've talked to,

00:14:05.190 --> 00:14:07.240
I haven't visited his classroom yet.

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:09.023
The teachers will spend
some time with the students

00:14:09.023 --> 00:14:10.770
that are learning at
their own time and pace

00:14:10.770 --> 00:14:13.190
on Khan Academy, I don't
think it's a bad idea

00:14:13.190 --> 00:14:14.850
to start all of your
students at kindergarten

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:16.650
and let them progress
as quickly as possible.

00:14:16.650 --> 00:14:19.270
Or early learning in
arithmetic, and let them

00:14:19.270 --> 00:14:20.930
progress as quickly as possible,

00:14:20.930 --> 00:14:23.470
maybe simultaneously as
they do the grade level.

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:26.780
What Tim has done is he
has all the students start

00:14:26.780 --> 00:14:29.410
at early math, but then he also assigns,

00:14:29.410 --> 00:14:31.150
let's say they're sixth
graders or seventh graders,

00:14:31.150 --> 00:14:33.360
he will use our assignments functionality

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:37.980
to assign unit one in sixth
grade, and then unit two.

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:40.060
And what he does is has
a double expectation

00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:40.893
for his students.

00:14:40.893 --> 00:14:43.520
He says, remediate, fill in those gaps

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:45.130
as quickly as you can,
if you don't have gaps,

00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:47.360
it'll happen quite
quickly, and simultaneously

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:48.500
work on the grade level stuff,

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:52.950
and I expect you to be done
1/4 through the grade 1/4

00:14:52.950 --> 00:14:54.550
through the year,
halfway through the grade

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:55.747
halfway through the year.

00:14:55.747 --> 00:14:58.050
He makes his grading based on that,

00:14:58.050 --> 00:14:59.540
but it's a mastery learning framework.

00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:01.330
So if a student's not
quite there at the quarter

00:15:01.330 --> 00:15:03.080
year mark, but then gets there later on,

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:05.400
he actually modifies
their grade to reflect

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:07.520
that they have now gotten to that point.

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:08.980
And that seems to work
really well for him.

00:15:08.980 --> 00:15:10.630
And the way it flows in the classroom,

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:14.110
when class is in session,
is he's able to look

00:15:14.110 --> 00:15:16.370
at the data of what
students are working on,

00:15:16.370 --> 00:15:18.050
who's engaged, who's not engaged.

00:15:18.050 --> 00:15:19.410
Obviously he can see some of that just by

00:15:19.410 --> 00:15:22.660
looking at the classroom,
and what are students

00:15:22.660 --> 00:15:23.610
having trouble with?

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:25.540
And then he can use that to take a handful

00:15:25.540 --> 00:15:27.970
of students to do a more
focused intervention,

00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:29.640
or maybe pair two students together

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:31.210
who are trying to work on the same skill,

00:15:31.210 --> 00:15:33.000
or maybe pair a student
who's already mastered

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:35.940
a concept with a student who's
trying to master the concept.

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:38.110
And what you find is it's
a very rich classroom.

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:40.440
A lot of people imagine
that online learning

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:42.440
can be kind of this Vulcan or Borg reality

00:15:42.440 --> 00:15:44.750
where kids are just on
computers and learning,

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:49.750
and in their isolated
places, but done well,

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:51.870
it can be highly, highly interactive.

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:54.780
And the teacher's able
to form good connections

00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:56.330
with the students,
students are able to talk,

00:15:56.330 --> 00:15:57.500
and interact, and teach each other.

00:15:57.500 --> 00:15:59.500
And obviously, you learn things the best

00:15:59.500 --> 00:16:00.844
when you actually have to teach it.

00:16:00.844 --> 00:16:03.378
Now that we're in this virtual world,

00:16:03.378 --> 00:16:06.454
I've heard of teachers and parents

00:16:06.454 --> 00:16:08.680
emulating aspects of that where the kids

00:16:08.680 --> 00:16:11.420
are working on Khan Academy,
and then simultaneously

00:16:11.420 --> 00:16:16.250
you might have a Google Hangout
or a Zoom conference call,

00:16:16.250 --> 00:16:18.740
or a Skype session at the same time

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:21.368
where students know that
they can go onto that

00:16:21.368 --> 00:16:25.665
and access their peers, or
their teacher, or parent

00:16:25.665 --> 00:16:27.880
who might be able to help them.

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:30.370
And you could imagine, in
this more virtual world

00:16:30.370 --> 00:16:32.020
that we're finding ourselves in,

00:16:32.020 --> 00:16:35.620
a parent, or a teacher,
or even an older student

00:16:35.620 --> 00:16:37.807
could look at the dashboard still and say,

00:16:37.807 --> 00:16:40.227
"Hey, looks like five of
y'all are really having

00:16:40.227 --> 00:16:41.400
"trouble with negative numbers,"

00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:43.317
and yes, the Khan Academy has resources,

00:16:43.317 --> 00:16:45.157
and that person could
say, "Hey, make sure you

00:16:45.157 --> 00:16:46.557
"watch the videos, make sure you look

00:16:46.557 --> 00:16:48.400
"at the explanations on the hints,"

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:51.217
But they could say, "Hey, at 10 a.m.,

00:16:51.217 --> 00:16:53.167
"I'm gonna run a 20 minute session,

00:16:53.167 --> 00:16:55.987
"a break out session on negative numbers

00:16:55.987 --> 00:16:59.150
"for you five to really
help you through it."

00:16:59.150 --> 00:17:02.927
Or you might see, "Hey, Khan Academy

00:17:02.927 --> 00:17:04.630
"does certain types of
items and problems,"

00:17:04.630 --> 00:17:07.300
but there might be a
really rich, multi step

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:09.707
application problem that you can say,

00:17:09.707 --> 00:17:11.327
"All right, half the students keep working

00:17:11.327 --> 00:17:13.127
"on Khan Academy, but
half of y'all are ready

00:17:13.127 --> 00:17:15.297
"for this deeper type of question,

00:17:15.297 --> 00:17:16.460
"let's work on that together,"

00:17:16.460 --> 00:17:18.210
and you could do it on
a Hangout or a Zoom.

00:17:18.210 --> 00:17:20.480
So those are the types of frameworks,

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:22.900
but we would love to hear
from all of you teachers

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:23.900
how you're using it.

00:17:24.940 --> 00:17:27.127
So Nadia Ali says, "Hi Sal, my son asks,

00:17:27.127 --> 00:17:29.247
"what is the importance
of the points you collect?

00:17:29.247 --> 00:17:31.187
"I answered the more points you score,

00:17:31.187 --> 00:17:35.260
"the smarter you are, but he
wants to hear it from you."

00:17:35.260 --> 00:17:37.920
Well I'll give a slightly
different answer,

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:40.293
what I would say is, the more you learn,

00:17:41.442 --> 00:17:44.550
the richer your life will be, so that's

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:46.230
the number one thing to remember.

00:17:46.230 --> 00:17:49.351
The points really are just points.

00:17:49.351 --> 00:17:50.740
(Sal laughs)

00:17:50.740 --> 00:17:53.840
They do help unlock things like avatars,

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:55.980
and things like that, but you know,

00:17:55.980 --> 00:17:57.240
the way our brains are wired,

00:17:57.240 --> 00:17:58.550
or many of our brains are wired,

00:17:58.550 --> 00:18:00.930
things like points, and I
think even in adulthood,

00:18:00.930 --> 00:18:02.700
many people are still
looking to score points

00:18:02.700 --> 00:18:06.220
in different ways, whether you
call a bank account points,

00:18:06.220 --> 00:18:09.039
or beyond a certain
measure, it probably is.

00:18:09.039 --> 00:18:12.230
But you know, those are just what's known

00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:15.190
as extrinsic motivators,
things that will hopefully

00:18:15.190 --> 00:18:16.610
motivate you to get more engaged.

00:18:16.610 --> 00:18:18.670
But the best motivators
are intrinsic motivators,

00:18:18.670 --> 00:18:20.860
when you realize that the best thing to do

00:18:20.860 --> 00:18:23.840
is just build that learning,
and maybe you could

00:18:23.840 --> 00:18:25.810
view the points, we have energy points,

00:18:25.810 --> 00:18:27.990
we have mastery points,
you can see your skills

00:18:27.990 --> 00:18:30.230
that you've leveled up,
that's a way to reflect

00:18:30.230 --> 00:18:31.710
on what you've learned.

00:18:31.710 --> 00:18:34.780
And that's actually the
most enriching thing of all,

00:18:34.780 --> 00:18:37.330
more than points or anything else.

00:18:37.330 --> 00:18:38.480
But that's what the points are,

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:40.620
they're indicative of what you've learned.

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:42.010
And I wouldn't even say necessarily smart

00:18:42.010 --> 00:18:43.162
or not smart.

00:18:43.162 --> 00:18:46.130
There was a question earlier
about the growth mindset,

00:18:46.130 --> 00:18:48.533
actually that's the next
question from Mahalia Lickich,

00:18:49.490 --> 00:18:52.188
I apologize if I'm mispronouncing
your name, Mahalia,

00:18:52.188 --> 00:18:55.330
but the whole principle of growth mindset

00:18:55.330 --> 00:18:57.620
is there's people with
either a fixed mindset

00:18:57.620 --> 00:18:58.453
or a growth mindset.

00:18:58.453 --> 00:19:00.130
Fixed mindset and the domain thinks,

00:19:00.130 --> 00:19:01.620
I'm either smart or I'm not.

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:04.237
And a growth mindset says,
"Well, I don't really know

00:19:04.237 --> 00:19:05.957
"my potential unless I'm always willing

00:19:05.957 --> 00:19:07.457
"to step out of my comfort zone,

00:19:07.457 --> 00:19:09.497
"try things that are difficult for me,

00:19:09.497 --> 00:19:11.417
"recognize that when I fail at something,

00:19:11.417 --> 00:19:12.587
"that's when I'm growing the most,

00:19:12.587 --> 00:19:14.710
"that's my opportunities for growth."

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:17.147
And when people have a fixed mindset,

00:19:17.147 --> 00:19:19.280
if they think they're smart at something,

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:24.280
many times they don't
want to try hard things

00:19:24.790 --> 00:19:26.870
because it might undermine
their self-perception,

00:19:26.870 --> 00:19:29.000
and if they think they're
not smart at something,

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:29.937
they don't wanna try
it, 'cause they're like,

00:19:29.937 --> 00:19:31.130
"Well I'm never gonna be good at it."

00:19:31.130 --> 00:19:32.625
But people with a growth
mindset will always say,

00:19:32.625 --> 00:19:35.327
"Hey, that's a challenge,
I might not succeed,

00:19:35.327 --> 00:19:37.770
"but I'm gonna try it,
and I'll work on it."

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:40.120
So really the points are there to reward

00:19:40.120 --> 00:19:41.810
and show how much effort you've put in,

00:19:41.810 --> 00:19:43.510
and how much you've been
willing to push yourself,

00:19:43.510 --> 00:19:44.900
and that I think is the muscle

00:19:44.900 --> 00:19:45.790
that we all wanna build.

00:19:45.790 --> 00:19:47.980
And if you look in the world, the people

00:19:47.980 --> 00:19:49.570
who are disproportionately successful

00:19:49.570 --> 00:19:50.880
are the people with growth mindsets.

00:19:50.880 --> 00:19:53.340
The people who are always
looking to improve themselves,

00:19:53.340 --> 00:19:54.987
push themselves, and aren't trying to say,

00:19:54.987 --> 00:19:57.257
"I'm smart or not smart,
just I'm gonna keep seeing

00:19:57.257 --> 00:19:58.317
"what I can do."

00:19:58.317 --> 00:19:59.920
(Sal laughs)

00:19:59.920 --> 00:20:04.327
So Hafsa Savilkia says,
"Anything that we can do

00:20:04.327 --> 00:20:05.160
"as a volunteer?

00:20:05.160 --> 00:20:06.127
"We'd love to be part of the team,

00:20:06.127 --> 00:20:07.657
"thank you for all you do."

00:20:09.530 --> 00:20:13.980
So there's multiple ways
you might volunteer.

00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:18.360
We have people for languages,
people for subtitling,

00:20:18.360 --> 00:20:21.570
I think in this time
that we find ourselves in

00:20:21.570 --> 00:20:24.760
of the school closures,
if you're in a position,

00:20:24.760 --> 00:20:27.350
find some kinds that you
might know in your community

00:20:27.350 --> 00:20:29.090
that might need help,
contact some teachers,

00:20:29.090 --> 00:20:32.090
especially kids who might
not have a lot of resources.

00:20:32.090 --> 00:20:34.280
One try to figure out how
to get them resources,

00:20:34.280 --> 00:20:36.810
I think computers and internet
are a lifeline right now,

00:20:36.810 --> 00:20:39.330
not just for accessing
things like Khan Academy,

00:20:39.330 --> 00:20:41.170
but just to be able to connect with people

00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:42.750
now that we all have to
be socially distanced.

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:44.670
So if you could help someway in that,

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:46.760
that could be really
powerful for the world.

00:20:46.760 --> 00:20:51.321
I think if you could
run video conferencing

00:20:51.321 --> 00:20:54.610
tutoring sessions, classes for people

00:20:54.610 --> 00:20:56.630
who wanna learn in your area.

00:20:56.630 --> 00:20:58.110
We're looking at ways
that we could kind of

00:20:58.110 --> 00:21:00.033
scale that type of thing up.

00:21:00.033 --> 00:21:02.020
We've recommended on our schedules

00:21:02.020 --> 00:21:04.080
that people do that, I've
already mentioned it,

00:21:04.080 --> 00:21:05.560
teachers, parents, older students

00:21:05.560 --> 00:21:08.630
are able to organize,
create video conferencing

00:21:08.630 --> 00:21:09.990
places where people can go get help.

00:21:09.990 --> 00:21:12.440
I think that could be a great
way to volunteer right now.

00:21:12.440 --> 00:21:14.300
I think there's ways
unrelated to Khan Academy

00:21:14.300 --> 00:21:16.920
that could be great to volunteer now.

00:21:16.920 --> 00:21:18.670
Find people who might not
have a lot of connection

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:21.030
to other people, get
on Hangouts with them,

00:21:21.030 --> 00:21:23.080
talk to them, I think people need that.

00:21:23.080 --> 00:21:25.300
I think if there's elderly
people in your area,

00:21:25.300 --> 00:21:26.920
offer to go buy their groceries for them,

00:21:26.920 --> 00:21:28.370
drop it off on their porches.

00:21:28.370 --> 00:21:29.610
I think that could be of great service.

00:21:29.610 --> 00:21:32.400
So there's a lot of opportunity
for service right now.

00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:34.380
But in the academic realm,
and Khan Academy realm,

00:21:34.380 --> 00:21:37.640
I'd say right now, run
tutoring sessions for folks.

00:21:37.640 --> 00:21:38.700
And when you do those tutoring sessions

00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:41.661
on Hangouts, or on a zoom session,

00:21:41.661 --> 00:21:45.010
some of it might be
explaining the concept,

00:21:45.010 --> 00:21:46.367
but a lot of it might just be motivating.

00:21:46.367 --> 00:21:48.197
"Hey, remember, you
could go to Khan Academy,

00:21:48.197 --> 00:21:49.327
"that's the video.

00:21:49.327 --> 00:21:50.930
"Hey, are you using it right?"

00:21:50.930 --> 00:21:52.380
And they can sign you up as their coach,

00:21:52.380 --> 00:21:54.050
so you can monitor and see how they're

00:21:54.050 --> 00:21:55.275
able to interact.

00:21:55.275 --> 00:21:56.108
You could say, "Hey,
so-and-so, it looks like

00:21:56.108 --> 00:21:57.147
"you haven't been working on it,

00:21:57.147 --> 00:21:57.980
"what's going on?"

00:21:57.980 --> 00:21:58.813
"How can I help you?"

00:21:58.813 --> 00:22:02.020
I think that by itself is
a huge, huge, huge service.

00:22:02.020 --> 00:22:04.560
And we are running, I forgot to mention,

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:06.223
parent webinars and teacher webinars

00:22:06.223 --> 00:22:08.440
to help parents and teachers understand

00:22:08.440 --> 00:22:09.820
how to use our tools and our reports

00:22:09.820 --> 00:22:10.653
a little bit more.

00:22:10.653 --> 00:22:13.350
So stay tuned for some of that.

00:22:13.350 --> 00:22:18.350
So I have Somol Sahu says,
"What is your typical day like?"

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:21.553
Well, I think that changes depending on

00:22:21.553 --> 00:22:23.183
whether you talk about the last week,

00:22:23.183 --> 00:22:25.928
or before the last week.

00:22:25.928 --> 00:22:29.053
This week has been a very
interesting week, for everyone.

00:22:31.520 --> 00:22:34.060
And I feel lucky that Khan
Academy's in a position

00:22:34.060 --> 00:22:36.070
to help the situation.

00:22:36.070 --> 00:22:37.780
Because as I said in other livestreams,

00:22:37.780 --> 00:22:39.461
there's three things that
people are worried about,

00:22:39.461 --> 00:22:41.970
their health, mental and physical health

00:22:41.970 --> 00:22:43.130
first and foremost.

00:22:43.130 --> 00:22:45.480
