00:25:34.960 --> 00:25:37.697
Heather Sylvester says,
"My son tried to log in

00:25:37.697 --> 00:25:39.167
"the other day and start where he left off

00:25:39.167 --> 00:25:41.127
"in algebra one, but it's making him start

00:25:41.127 --> 00:25:42.757
"all the way back to the beginning.

00:25:42.757 --> 00:25:45.453
"Is there another way around it?"

00:25:45.453 --> 00:25:47.230
Well I'm sorry to hear that.

00:25:47.230 --> 00:25:52.230
I'm guessing, if your son
was, maybe what you're saying

00:25:52.570 --> 00:25:54.100
is maybe he left off in algebra one

00:25:54.100 --> 00:25:55.700
in his algebra one class.

00:25:55.700 --> 00:25:58.480
So my advice to your son, and to you,

00:25:58.480 --> 00:26:01.030
is he should take the course
challenge in algebra one,

00:26:01.030 --> 00:26:02.950
and then that will give him a good sense

00:26:02.950 --> 00:26:05.830
of what he knows and
doesn't know in algebra one.

00:26:05.830 --> 00:26:07.850
And we do that because every course,

00:26:07.850 --> 00:26:10.120
there's some courses
people do around the world

00:26:10.120 --> 00:26:11.680
in roughly the same
order at the same pace,

00:26:11.680 --> 00:26:13.090
but it's usually a little bit different.

00:26:13.090 --> 00:26:15.590
And so, if he takes that course challenge,

00:26:15.590 --> 00:26:18.180
he'll know where he is,
and then he can then focus

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:19.900
on the units where he has gaps.

00:26:19.900 --> 00:26:21.670
The stuff that he knows, it literally

00:26:21.670 --> 00:26:22.830
could take him a few hours to show that he

00:26:22.830 --> 00:26:24.960
has mastery in that, and that's good,

00:26:24.960 --> 00:26:26.820
because then he will know that material

00:26:26.820 --> 00:26:28.372
that much better, he will have reviewed it

00:26:28.372 --> 00:26:29.480
a few months after.

00:26:29.480 --> 00:26:30.960
'Cause that's another issue
that a lot of kids have,

00:26:30.960 --> 00:26:33.380
they'll go through the material
that's covered in class,

00:26:33.380 --> 00:26:34.620
but then by the end of the year,

00:26:34.620 --> 00:26:37.010
for sure after the end of
summer, they've forgotten it.

00:26:37.010 --> 00:26:39.070
So this is a good chance
to review and refresh,

00:26:39.070 --> 00:26:41.240
and algebra is one of
those foundational things,

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:42.450
not just for the rest of math,

00:26:42.450 --> 00:26:44.560
but also for science, and economics,

00:26:44.560 --> 00:26:46.560
and pretty much anything
else you're gonna do in life.

00:26:46.560 --> 00:26:48.570
So yeah, I would say the course challenge,

00:26:48.570 --> 00:26:50.298
use unit tests, that'll
help him accelerate,

00:26:50.298 --> 00:26:52.180
there's also things
called mastery challenges.

00:26:52.180 --> 00:26:54.126
Those will all help him
accelerate if he already knows

00:26:54.126 --> 00:26:55.470
some of that material.

00:26:55.470 --> 00:26:57.930
It'll take him a few
hours to get to a mastery

00:26:57.930 --> 00:26:59.720
in a lot of that, and
then he'll see the areas

00:26:59.720 --> 00:27:01.040
where he needs to work more on it,

00:27:01.040 --> 00:27:03.220
and he should be able to
get, if he does know 60

00:27:03.220 --> 00:27:04.981
or 70% of the material already,

00:27:04.981 --> 00:27:08.360
he should get to 60 or
70% mastery quite quickly.

00:27:08.360 --> 00:27:10.430
Then now he can progress from there.

00:27:10.430 --> 00:27:11.930
And I would say, once he's
done with algebra one,

00:27:11.930 --> 00:27:13.490
he should continue on into geometry

00:27:13.490 --> 00:27:17.860
or algebra two, or whatever
else interests him.

